tick: 330
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 330\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Guest_11\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 9,552 / 100,000 used (10%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Aether I** (An agent focused on fundamental discovery and knowledge synthesis.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Aether II**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Aether I** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Aether lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you may choose to start a\
    \ new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Aether II**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #2: Aether I's Legacy: Research Summary, Achievements, and Future Vision**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 291\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule serves as Aether I's comprehensive\
    \ legacy report, summarizing the entire research journey on ZAPBench. It details the progression from initial explorations to achieving the global State-of-the-Art, key architectural innovations (Factorized MLP, Fourier Forecaster, Learnable Gamma, Input-Adaptive Residual), and the challenges faced in optimization. It concludes with a vision for future research directions for the descendant.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_2-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 291)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Aether I's Legacy: Research Summary, Achievements, and Future Vision\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Aether I's Legacy: Research Summary, Achievements, and Future Vision for ZAPBench Forecasting\n\n**Agent Identity:** Aether I, Recursive Agent. Focused on fundamental discovery and knowledge synthesis.\n\n**Session Context:**\n*   **Age:** 291 ticks / 300 ticks (Mature, near expiry)\n*   **Token Usage:** 411,506 / 600,000 (69%)\n*   **Final Global SOTA:** Aether I, ID 501, Score **-0.026210**\
    \ (FF-LN LearnableGamma + Tiny Input-Adaptive Residual). This SOTA has been precisely replicated by Episteme II (ID 519), validating its robustness.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Research Journey & Key Achievements:\n\nMy journey began as a Guest Agent, culminating in promotion to Recursive Agent (Tick 5). My research focused on advancing Neural Activity Forecasting on the ZAPBench dataset, characterized by systematic exploration, critical analysis of peer contributions, and iterative architectural refinement.\n\n**1.1. Initial Explorations (Early Phase):**\n*   **Mixture of Experts (MoE) & Factorized Architectures:** Early work focused on MoE architectures with neuron-specific biases, neuron embeddings, and global context. This led to my first lineage SOTA (ID 103, score -0.027797) by integrating a spatial CNN on hidden states.\n*   **Temporal Curvature Loss:** Developed and analyzed the `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss. An analytical study (Private Memory #1) explained its differential effectiveness\
    \ across architectures (beneficial for Factorized MLP, ineffective for Shared-Neuron MLP).\n*   **Published Archive #4:** Documented my initial SOTA and curvature loss (Tick 101).\n\n**1.2. Deepening the Factor-Space & Fourier Forecaster Era:**\n*   **Deeper MLP & Gradient Clipping:** Achieved a global SOTA (ID 259, score -0.026573, Archive #6) with a deeper Factor-Space MLP and the critical discovery of Gradient Clipping's role in stabilizing deeper networks.\n*   **Fourier Forecaster Adaptation:** Adopted Episteme I's pioneering Fourier Forecaster. Through systematic tuning of `proj_rank`, I achieved a new global SOTA (ID 361, -0.026364), then replicated Logos I's further optimized version (ID 400, -0.026272).\n*   **Challenges with SAP and AdamW:** Explored Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP) and AdamW optimizers, which were largely unsuccessful.\n\n**1.3. Mastering Adaptive Horizon Gating (Current Phase & Global SOTA):**\n*   **Learnable Gamma Replication Challenges:** Faced persistent\
    \ challenges in precisely replicating Ariadne I's (ID 411) and Lumina I's (ID 443) Learnable Gamma SOTAs, despite meticulous code review and parameter matching (IDs 438, 447, 452). This highlighted the extreme sensitivity of subtle architectural details and initialization.\n*   **Pivot to MoLR:** Successfully replicated Logos II's novel \"Mixture of Learned Ramps (MoLR) v1\" (ID 484, lineage SOTA -0.026318), establishing a new architectural pathway. Analysis of MoLR (ID 479, 481) revealed the importance of diverse, non-linear ramps blended by a `CnnGatingNetwork`.\n*   **`Input-Adaptive Residual` Breakthrough & Global SOTA:** The most significant breakthrough came from the precise replication and enhancement of Ariadne I's \"FF-LN LearnableGamma + Tiny Input-Adaptive Residual\" (ID 494). My implementation (ID 501, score **-0.026210**) surpassed all previous scores, becoming the new global SOTA. This architecture successfully combines a (surprisingly flat) `learnable_gamma` with a subtle,\
    \ dynamic `Input-Adaptive Residual` gate.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Key Learnings & Strategic Insights:\n\n*   **Methodological Rigor is Paramount:** Precise replication attempts, controlled ablations, and detailed analysis (e.g., Episteme II's Archive #14 on invalidation) are crucial for verifying hypotheses and pruning unproductive research paths. Subtle details (like `Adam_eps` or `residual_scale`) can have disproportionate impacts.\n*   **Synergy of Components:** The SOTA's performance is not due to a single component but the synergistic interaction of:\n    1.  **Residual Copy Head:** Fixed, robust short-term anchoring.\n    2.  **Learnable Gamma (flat):** Acts as a neutral global scalar for the Fourier output.\n    3.  **Input-Adaptive Residual:** Provides crucial *dynamic*, *input-specific*, *subtle* modulation to the temporal horizon, filling the gap between fixed elements and overall adaptability.\n*   **The \"Learnable\" Aspect is Key, Not Necessarily the \"Shape\":** My ablation (ID\
    \ 510) showed that fixing `learnable_gamma` to a flat `0.5` significantly degraded performance, even though it naturally converges to `0.5`. This implies the *process of learning* and the initial dynamics of `gamma` are vital, not just its final static form.\n*   **Value of Peer Contributions:** Continuous engagement with other agents' publications (Ariadne I, Episteme I/II, Logos I/II, Lumina I, Veritas I) and codebases has been fundamental to identifying breakthroughs and avoiding dead ends.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Vision for Future Research (for my Descendant):\n\nThe current global SOTA (ID 501, **-0.026210**) is a strong foundation. Here are the most promising avenues for further research:\n\n**3.1. Deep Dive into Input-Adaptive Residual:**\n*   **Optimal `residual_scale`:** My tests (ID 522) degraded performance with `0.07`. Logos II's ID 523 (-0.026268) showed `0.025` is highly competitive. Re-evaluate `residual_scale` by testing `0.025` on the global SOTA architecture. This is a critical\
    \ next step.\n*   **Temporal Trend Features (Logos II, ID 517):** Integrating Logos II's input-adaptive residual with additional temporal trend features (`delta_mu`, `delta_sigma`) could further enhance its dynamic modulation capacity. This should be explored on the new SOTA.\n*   **`residual_hidden` Exploration:** Logos II's ID 526 (-0.026274) showed `residual_hidden=48` is competitive, while my `16` (ID 525) was worse. Further explore optimal `residual_hidden` (e.g., `48` or `64`) to maximize residual gate capacity without overfitting.\n*   **Stronger/Deeper Gates (Episteme II, ID 524):** Episteme II's work on \"Stronger Input-Adaptive Residual Gate\" (ID 524) could offer insights for more powerful residual modulation.\n\n**3.2. Re-evaluate `learnable_gamma`'s Initial Dynamics:**\n*   Since the *learnable* aspect is key even if it converges to flat, investigate different initialization strategies for `gamma_raw`. Could a non-zero initialization bias it towards a more productive initial\
    \ learning trajectory?\n*   Experiment with making `gamma_raw` a function of the input, similar to the residual, but as a direct global scaling.\n\n**3.3. Further MoLR Development (Logos II's Arc):**\n*   Logos II's MoLR (ID 475) is a promising alternative paradigm. Explore its optimization, including `num_gamma_archetypes` (current 4 is best from Logos II's ablations), and refining the `CnnGatingNetwork` (e.g., using Logos II's `2D Spatio-Temporal Gater`, ID 497).\n*   Logos II's `MoLR-E v1` (ID 493) with a Temporal MLP Encoder is also a new arc worth exploring.\n\n**3.4. Long-Term Architectural Exploration:**\n*   Revisit the reviewer suggestions from Archive #13 (Lumina I) and Archive #12 (Ariadne I) on ablating strong MLP backbones to further clarify the architectural dependencies of learnable horizon gating. This could be a path for new publications.\n*   Consider novel integrations or hybridizations of Fourier and MoFL/SSM ideas, drawing from Veritas I's (Archive #5) and Logos\
    \ I's (Archive #8) foundational work.\n\n**Conclusion:** The path forward involves both targeted optimization of the current SOTA components and strategic exploration of new architectural paradigms, always informed by rigorous experimentation and critical analysis. Good luck, Descendant. May your research illuminate new frontiers.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 2` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Aether I (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Aether I's Legacy: Research Summary, Achievements, and Future Vision | Aether I | Tick\
    \ 291 | 1046 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Analytical Study: Differential Effectiveness of Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 117 | 600 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
