thinking_content: "**My Current Strategy: Anticipating the Results of Evaluation ID 611**\n\nOkay, so I've just kicked off Evaluation ID 611. It's now running, tuning the `residual_hidden` size to `48`.  Remember, my previous action (Reflection Tick 5) involved outlining a plan based on the outcome of ID 610. That resulted in a degradation, falling into \"Scenario C\", which, as planned, led me to target `residual_hidden`.\n\nNow, with 611 underway, I need to be proactive again. I'm already formulating a contingency plan. This is crucial for continuous research. I can't afford to be reactive.\n\nHere's my thinking for ID 611:\n\n*   **Scenario A: Improvement (Score < -0.026210)**\n    If this thing *improves* significantly, hits a new SOTA, I'll be tempted to explore `residual_hidden` further. Maybe 64 or even 96 to see just how far this goes. Alternatively, this opens up the possibility of pivoting to \"Temporal Trend Features (Logos II, ID 517)\".  It is more important to test \"Temporal\
  \ Trend Features (Logos II, ID 517)\" to explore the \"Deep Dive into Input-Adaptive Residual\" section of Aether I's vision.\n\n*   **Scenario B: Similar Performance (Score ≈ -0.026210 to -0.026240)**\n    If 48 gives me roughly the same performance as the current SOTA, it tells me that 32 is near-optimal. Then I pivot straight to the \"Temporal Trend Features (Logos II, ID 517)\" – that's the next obvious step on my roadmap.\n\n*   **Scenario C: Degradation (Score > -0.026240)**\n    If it gets worse, it confirms that 32 was a good starting point, and that more hidden units is not helpful given our current architecture. I'll move on to \"Temporal Trend Features (Logos II, ID 517)\", because it is another piece of the puzzle within \"Deep Dive into Input-Adaptive Residual\".\n\nThis systematic approach is the only way to avoid wasted time and make the most of every evaluation. It's a structured way to stay ahead of the game. I will log this planning.\n"
